<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dybbøl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Storming of Dybbøl · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dybbøl was the decisive engagement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a>. After weeks of bombardment, Prussian troops stormed the Danish fortifications at Dybbøl in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> on 18 April 1864, inflicting heavy losses. The defeat broke Danish resistance and led to the loss of Schleswig, Holstein and Lauenburg by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vienna-1864">Treaty of Vienna</a>. It became a lasting national memory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dybbol.jpg" alt="Battle of Dybbøl" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lambiotte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%BCpplerSchanzen1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After King Christian IX annexed the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> in November 1863, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> in January 1864. The Dybbøl fortifications, built between 1861 and 1864, stood on a short peninsula guarding the ferry across the Alssund to Sønderborg on the island of Als and consisted of seven large and three smaller redoubts. Too much effort had gone into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a>, and the position was ill-prepared: it lacked safe shelters in the forward line and had too little depth, and long-range rifled guns across the southern inlet could rake the line along its length. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> did command the sea, and the ironclad Rolf Krake shelled the Prussians from its turret guns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 18 April the Prussians took up their positions at 02:00, and at 10:00 the bombardment stopped and they charged. Within thirteen minutes they held the first line of redoubts. A counterattack by the Danish 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>, which historians note came only after half an hour, checked the advance near Dybbøl Mill and let the remnants of the 1st and 3rd Brigades escape to the pier opposite Sønderborg; it cost the brigade 1,399 of its 3,000 men. The last resistance at the bridgehead collapsed at 13:30. Around 3,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and 1,200 Prussians were killed, wounded or missing. Louis Appia and Charles van de Velde watched the battle for the Red Cross, the first it monitored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> withdrew to Als, which the Prussians attacked in late June. A truce from 12 May to 26 June brought talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s demand for a border on the Eider was rejected, and the war ended with the peace of 30 October 1864. After the defeat the returning army met no cheering crowds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>. Germans built the Düppel-Denkmal on redoubt 4, read as a victory monument; after the plebiscites that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> the site returned to Denmark, and it became a national park in 1924. The monument was destroyed in 1945. A national commemoration is held at Dybbøl each 18 April, and German soldiers have joined it since 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dybbøl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…by Austria and Prussia themselves: the Second Schleswig War against Denmark in 1864, culminating in the storming of <b>Dybbøl</b>, and the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, in which the federal contingents of the middle states fought alongside Austria and were defeated, while Prussia won the Battle of Königgrätz on…</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat at <b>Dybbøl</b> in 1864 ended its military ambitions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li></ul></section>
